 ### [Scene Parameterization Radius](15160-scene-parameterization-radius.md)
-Constrains large-scale unbounded street-view scenes into a bounded unit sphere volume to enable efficient voxel grid allocation and position encoding, avoiding the heavy range compression of close objects inherent in standard Normalized Device Coordinates (NDC) or basic contraction methods. It sets apart from its siblings by using a piecewise mapping function controlled by a radius parameter $r$ to preserve high-frequency details for nearby objects while mapping distant geometry, specifically tailored for ego-vehicle scenarios where camera motion complicates standard NDC assumptions. Choose for dynamic street-view or ego-vehicle rendering.
+Constrains large-scale unbounded street-view scenes into a bounded range using a piecewise distance mapping function controlled by a radius parameter $r$ to preserve details for close objects. It differs from standard Normalized Device Coordinates (NDC) by decoupling near-field resolution from far-field bounds, which is necessary for ego-vehicle scenarios with complex camera motion. Choose for dynamic street-view or ego-vehicle rendering.
 
-### [Piecewise-Projective Contraction](10198-piecewise-projective-contraction.md)
-A method to handle unbounded forward-facing scenes by warping the frustum to a bounded volume using a dense voxel grid allocation similar to Multiplane Images (MPIs). It places $D$ RGB-density images at fixed depths, each with $X \times Z$ resolution, and traces every ray from the first to the $D$-th images with a step size of $s$ image, resulting in $2D - 1$ sampled points if $s = 0.5$. Choose for forward-facing or indoor corridor scenes where depth layers align with camera motion.
+### Forward-facing scene parameterization
+A method to handle unbounded forward-facing scenes by warping the frustum to a bounded volume using a dense voxel grid allocation similar to Multiplane Images (MPIs) [S2, unofficial]. It places $D$ RGB-density images at fixed depths, each with $X \times Z$ resolution, and traces every ray from the first to the $D$-th images with a step size of $s$ image, resulting in $2D - 1$ sampled points if $s = 0.5$ [S2, unofficial]. Choose for forward-facing or indoor corridor scenes where depth layers align with camera motion.
 
-### [Piecewise-Projective Contraction](10198-piecewise-projective-contraction.md)
-A contraction function that maps unbounded space into a finite domain while preserving straight lines (piecewise-linear), allowing for efficient ray-AABB intersection tests for empty space skipping. It consists of seven regions (unit cube + 6 outer regions). Unlike spherical contraction which maps to a radius-2 ball, this contraction preserves the unit cube and maps other regions via projective transformations. Choose when empty space skipping and ray-box intersection efficiency are critical.
+### Initial Meshes for Scene Types
+Coordinate system definitions and transformations for different scene types [S5]. Synthetic 360° scenes apply scaling $p' = w p$ where $w=2.4$ or $3$ depending on object size [S5]. Forward-facing scenes apply exponential transformation to concentrate voxels near the camera [S5]. Unbounded 360° scenes use no transformation to the regular grid but add $L+1$ concentric boxes around the grid to model surrounding environments [S5]. Choose when initializing voxel grids or occupancy structures for specific scene topologies.
 
-### [Piecewise-Projective Contraction](10198-piecewise-projective-contraction.md)
-Coordinate system definitions and transformations for different scene types. Synthetic 360° scenes apply scaling $p' = w p$ where $w=2.4$ or $3$ depending on object size. Forward-facing scenes apply exponential transformation to concentrate voxels near the camera. Unbounded 360° scenes use no transformation to the regular grid but add $L+1$ concentric boxes around the grid to model surrounding environments. Choose when initializing voxel grids or occupancy structures for specific scene topologies.
+### Scene Parameterization
+For forward-facing scenes, parameterize scenes with normalized device coordinates (NDC). For unbounded scenes, parameterize using contraction parameterization [S6]. Decompose the voxel grid using VM-decomposition in TensoRF for model compactness [S6]. Add distortion loss to encourage solid surface scene reconstruction and prevent floaters [S6]. Choose when using TensoRF-style decomposition for unbounded wild video scenes.
 
